WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.797 --> 00:00:03.420
- [Narrator] Figure one, the horse.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.272
This is a story about a horse.

00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:06.221
(horse neighs)

00:00:06.221 --> 00:00:09.632
A horse whose fate was inextricably tied

00:00:09.632 --> 00:00:11.332
to the invention of film

00:00:11.332 --> 00:00:14.430
and it all started with
an innocent question.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.204
Do all four of a horses hooves

00:00:17.204 --> 00:00:21.037
leave the ground at any
point during a gallop?

00:00:24.728 --> 00:00:27.765
Leland Stanford, yes, the dignified chap

00:00:27.765 --> 00:00:30.797
who founded Stanford University

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.993
thought they did.

00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:34.617
- All four of the horses hooves

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:37.257
leave the ground while galloping.

00:00:37.257 --> 00:00:40.100
- [Narrator] But some other
academics thought they didn't.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.613
- One foot is most certainly
always touching the ground!

00:00:43.613 --> 00:00:45.648
- [Narrator] So to put the matter to rest

00:00:45.648 --> 00:00:48.650
Stanford hired a professional photographer

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:49.921
Eadweard Muybridge

00:00:49.921 --> 00:00:52.338
to capture the truth on film.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.825
There was a bet.

00:00:57.825 --> 00:00:59.773
Thousands of dollars were at stake

00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:02.433
as well as a man’s reputation.

00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:03.573
But wait!

00:01:03.573 --> 00:01:04.855
Cameras at the time could not

00:01:04.855 --> 00:01:07.080
capture things moving quickly.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.754
There was no such thing
as a movie camera in 1878.

00:01:11.754 --> 00:01:14.504
So Muybridge had to get creative.

00:01:15.647 --> 00:01:18.192
He devised an ingenious solution.

00:01:18.192 --> 00:01:22.591
12 wired triggers and 12
shutters for 12 cameras.

00:01:22.591 --> 00:01:24.187
All evenly spaced

00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:27.977
to capture a horse and
carriage on a racetrack.

00:01:27.977 --> 00:01:30.343
The carriage was driven
across the trip wires

00:01:30.343 --> 00:01:34.163
setting off all 12 cameras
in less than half a second

00:01:34.163 --> 00:01:35.830
capturing the truth.

00:01:37.241 --> 00:01:41.591
- You can clearly see all four
hooves have left the ground.

00:01:41.591 --> 00:01:42.655
(gasps)

00:01:42.655 --> 00:01:44.819
- [Narrator] Leland Stanford was right.

00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:49.145
But honestly, we don't
really care about the horse.

00:01:49.145 --> 00:01:51.018
What we do care about, however,

00:01:51.018 --> 00:01:53.913
is Muybridges newfangled contraption

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.563
used to photograph things in motion.

00:01:56.563 --> 00:01:58.597
See what I'm getting at here?

00:01:58.597 --> 00:02:01.997
Thomas Edison and the Lumière
brothers certainly did.

00:02:01.997 --> 00:02:03.631
They took Muybridges invention

00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:06.596
and changed the course
of media as we know it.

00:02:06.596 --> 00:02:09.712
Creating the motion picture.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:12.212
As for the academics who lost the bet?

00:02:12.212 --> 00:02:14.712
They're fine, they had tenure.

00:02:15.572 --> 00:02:18.155
(upbeat music)

